(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-asthma-vignettes"]={"title":"Asthma Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has asthma symptoms only 1 day a week and uses albuterol 1 day a week, but she wakes at night with cough twice a week. She has no activity limitation, and her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 86% predicted. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severity is assigned to the most severe category in which any feature occurs. Her daytime symptoms and lung function are intermittent-level, but night awakenings more than once a week (twice weekly) are a moderate persistent feature, so she is moderate persistent. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with asthma wakes with cough and mild wheeze. Her peak flow is 70% of her personal best, placing her in the yellow zone of her written action plan. What does the plan direct her to do first?","choices":["Start an oral steroid right away","Go to the hospital now","Add quick-relief medicine and keep her controller","Stop her controller until she feels better"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the yellow zone, 50% to 79% of personal best, asthma is getting worse: she adds quick-relief medicine (a short-acting beta-2 agonist) and keeps taking her green-zone controller. An oral steroid is added only if she does not return to green after an hour. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman with asthma records her peak flow at home. Over the past week her morning readings before albuterol have been about 280 L/min and her afternoon readings after albuterol about 380 L/min. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["Poor asthma control","Good asthma control","A faulty peak flow meter","Normal circadian variation needing no action"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peak flow is measured in the morning before and the afternoon after the bronchodilator, and a 20% change between them, or day to day, indicates poor control. A rise from 280 to 380 is about 36% of the morning value (26% of the afternoon value), well over the 20% line. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma was given salmeterol by itself at an urgent care visit and it has been her sole controller for a month. Which change does her regimen need?","choices":["Replace it with tiotropium","Add an inhaled corticosteroid","Continue salmeterol alone","Switch it to cromolyn sodium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A long-acting beta-2 agonist such as salmeterol has no anti-inflammatory effect and should not be used as monotherapy; it is used in combination with an inhaled corticosteroid. Adding one treats her airway inflammation and may allow control at a lower steroid dose. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with asthma had facial flushing, red eyes and wheezing several hours after taking aspirin last month. He now has low back pain from lifting. Which medication should he avoid?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Meloxicam","Celecoxib","Albuterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flushing, conjunctival irritation and wheeze after aspirin, even when delayed up to 12 hours, mark aspirin-sensitive asthma. Ibuprofen is a non-selective anti-inflammatory drug that cross-reacts and must be avoided; selective agents may be used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman with asthma has a personal best peak flow of 450 L/min. This morning she has cough and chest tightness, and her peak flow is 300 L/min (67% of her best). According to her written asthma action plan, which zone is she in?","choices":["Red zone","Not classifiable without spirometry","Yellow zone","Green zone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her reading of 300 of a best 450 is 67%, inside the yellow zone of 50% to 79% of personal best, where asthma is getting worse. The plan adds quick-relief medicine (a short-acting beta-2 agonist) while she keeps taking her green-zone controller. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has had episodes of wheezing, chest tightness and cough for a year, worse at night and with cold air. Her lung exam is normal today. Which test best confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Spirometry with a bronchodilator","Chest x-ray","Allergy skin testing","Fractional exhaled nitric oxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry is the objective measurement of lung function, with best results when measured before and after a short-acting bronchodilator. Reduced values that improve 12% or more after albuterol strongly support asthma, even when the exam is normal between episodes, as hers is. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 52, 53, 59"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with long-standing asthma has had repeated exacerbations this year despite confirmed adherence and good inhaler technique. Her clinician suspects a hypersensitivity reaction to a mold. Which result supports that suspicion?","choices":["A raised exhaled nitric oxide by itself","Raised immunoglobulin E and G to Aspergillus","Negative skin tests with a normal immunoglobulin E","Normal antibody levels to Aspergillus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus seen almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis. Exposure causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response that leads to acute symptoms and exacerbations, as in this woman. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has new-onset persistent wheezing and cough that began last year. She has nasal polyps. Allergy skin tests are negative and her serum immunoglobulin E is normal. She had a wheezing episode after taking aspirin for a headache. Which type of asthma is most likely?","choices":["Intrinsic asthma","Extrinsic asthma","Occupational asthma","Catamenial asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adult onset, persistent symptoms, nasal polyps, negative skin tests with a normal immunoglobulin E, and aspirin sensitivity all fit intrinsic asthma, the non-atopic form seen in about 10% of patients, which is usually more severe and persistent. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 35"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with moderate persistent asthma comes in for her yearly influenza vaccine, and her mother asks whether she can have the nasal spray form instead of a shot. Which vaccine form calls for caution in her?","choices":["Any influenza vaccine this year","The pneumonia vaccine","Every vaccine during asthma treatment","The intranasal influenza vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Annual influenza vaccination is recommended because vaccine-preventable illness can trigger asthma exacerbations, but intranasal influenza vaccines call for caution, since they can be associated with exacerbation in younger patients such as this 14-year-old. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who works in a furniture-finishing plant has had cough and wheezing for 6 months. She notes the symptoms ease on weekends and nearly vanish during a 2-week vacation, then return within days of going back to work. She has no history of allergies. Which diagnosis best explains her pattern?","choices":["Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Occupational asthma","Extrinsic allergic asthma","Catamenial asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms that improve on weekends and vacations and return at work point to occupational asthma from a workplace sensitizer, here chemicals in furniture finishing. Occupational sensitization is independent of atopy, so her lack of allergies does not argue against it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman is diagnosed with asthma at an initial visit, her severity is classified as mild persistent, and an inhaled corticosteroid is started with a demonstration of its use. Which step completes the initial visit before follow-up is scheduled?","choices":["Order a chest x-ray","Begin allergen immunotherapy","Refer her to a pulmonologist","Write an asthma action plan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial visit runs: diagnose asthma, assess severity, initiate medication and demonstrate its use, develop a written asthma action plan, then schedule follow-up. She has had the first 3, so the action plan comes next. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with asthma uses his albuterol inhaler several times a day, refills it every 2 weeks, and stopped his inhaled corticosteroid months ago. Which feature of his history most raises his risk of death from asthma?","choices":["Allergy to cat dander","Heavy rescue use without a steroid inhaler","Atopic dermatitis as a child","Asthma that began at 3 years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The risk of death from asthma rises with poorly controlled asthma shown by frequent bronchodilator use, no inhaled corticosteroids, poor compliance with them, and previous admissions for near-fatal attacks. His heavy albuterol use after stopping his steroid combines the first 3. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 14, 71"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with asthma took aspirin at noon for a headache and developed facial flushing, a runny nose and wheezing at about 10 that night. He asks whether the aspirin could be responsible so many hours later. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["No, aspirin sensitivity causes rash alone","Yes, but just selective agents cause it","Yes, reactions can be delayed up to 12 hours","No, reactions occur within minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In aspirin-sensitive asthma, symptoms may occur within minutes of aspirin ingestion or may be delayed up to 12 hours, and they include rhinorrhea, conjunctival irritation, facial flushing and wheezing. His reaction 10 hours after the dose fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has spirometry for suspected asthma. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is reduced with a reduced ratio, and it improves 14% after albuterol. How should this response be read?","choices":["As a positive bronchodilator response","As too small to count","As proof of fixed obstruction","As a reason to skip spirometry next time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Improvement of 12% or more in the forced expiratory volume in 1 second after a bronchodilator is a positive response, and her 14% rise meets it. A positive response on top of reduced baseline values strongly supports the diagnosis of asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 52, 53, 54"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with asthma takes ibuprofen for a sprained ankle. Within 20 minutes he develops a runny nose, red irritated eyes, facial flushing and wheezing. Which diagnosis explains the reaction?","choices":["Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Cardiac asthma","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Aspirin-sensitive asthma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 1 to 5% of asthmatics worsen with aspirin, and patients with true aspirin sensitivity cross-react with non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen. Rhinorrhea, conjunctival irritation, facial flushing and wheezing within minutes of the dose is the described reaction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 16-year-old has asthma symptoms 3 days a week, wakes at night 3 times a month, and uses albuterol 3 days a week, with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. Which long-term control medication is preferred for him?","choices":["Leukotriene modifier","Long-acting beta-2 agonist alone","Theophylline","Inhaled corticosteroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His symptoms and rescue use on more than 2 days a week, 3 night awakenings a month and 85% function make mild persistent asthma. Inhaled corticosteroids are the preferred first-line treatment for persistent asthma, and most patients respond to twice-daily dosing. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 62, 74"},{"q":"A 17-year-old with exercise-induced bronchospasm takes cromolyn sodium only after her wheezing starts during a game and finds that it does not help. Which explains the lack of relief?","choices":["She has become resistant to it","It works just for allergic triggers","It prevents but does not relieve symptoms","It is a bronchodilator with slow onset"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cromolyn is a mast cell stabilizer: it inhibits mast cell activation and eosinophil recruitment to prevent symptoms in mild persistent or exercise-induced asthma, but it does not relieve symptoms once present. Taking it after wheezing starts is too late. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with suspected asthma has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 58% predicted with a reduced ratio, and it rises only 6% after albuterol. A colleague proposes a methacholine challenge to look for airway hyper-responsiveness. Which test is inappropriate for her now?","choices":["Methacholine challenge","Allergy skin testing","Home peak flow monitoring","Chest x-ray"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial provocation testing is not recommended when the forced expiratory volume in 1 second is below 65% predicted, and hers is 58%. Provoking bronchoconstriction on top of that much obstruction is unsafe, so the challenge is the test to avoid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman in a severe asthma exacerbation sits leaning forward with hunched shoulders and uses her accessory muscles. Loud wheezing earlier has faded, and now she has only decreased breath sounds with a prolonged expiration. What does the loss of wheezing most likely indicate?","choices":["Resolution of the exacerbation","Vocal fold dysfunction","A response to her inhaler","Severe airflow obstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Wheezing may not be present in severe exacerbations, which may show only decreased breath sounds with prolonged expiration. With her accessory muscle use and posture, a quiet chest signals worse obstruction, not improvement. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 51"},{"q":"A 17-year-old runner with exercise-induced bronchospasm uses albuterol before every practice, yet still has frequent symptoms during training. Which change is most likely to prevent his episodes?","choices":["Regular inhaled corticosteroid use","Saving albuterol for after symptoms start","Cromolyn sodium taken during symptoms","Stopping exercise permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is treated with a short-acting beta-2 agonist before exercise but is best prevented with regular inhaled corticosteroid use. His frequent symptoms despite pre-exercise albuterol call for the regular controller. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 66, 96"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has had mild asthma since childhood, controlled with occasional albuterol. He asks whether his asthma is likely to become severe as he ages. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Mild asthma rarely becomes severe","Most mild asthma turns severe within a decade","Severity rises steadily with each decade","Severe asthma usually begins mild"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma severity rarely varies for a given patient: mild asthma rarely becomes severe, and severe asthma is usually severe at onset. His long history of mild disease predicts it will most likely stay mild. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with severe persistent asthma also has poorly controlled hypertension, osteoporosis, and a gastric ulcer last year. His asthma remains poorly controlled. Which medication's side effects most threaten his other conditions?","choices":["Short-acting beta-2 agonists","Leukotriene modifiers","Long-term oral corticosteroids","Inhaled corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroid side effects include hypertension, osteoporosis and gastric ulceration, all 3 of his other conditions, plus truncal obesity, bruising and depression. If he needs them, the minimum dose that gives control is used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 76, 77"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with newly diagnosed persistent asthma starts using a peak flow meter at home. During his first weeks of readings while well, what is the main purpose of the readings?","choices":["Measuring his forced vital capacity","Establishing his personal best baseline","Replacing spirometry for diagnosis","Identifying his allergen triggers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Home peak flow monitoring first establishes a baseline for the patient, his personal best, and then assesses control against it. His action-plan zones are set as percentages of that personal best, so the early readings are the foundation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 60, 88"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man with spirometry-confirmed asthma notices flares in spring and whenever he visits a friend with dogs. He wants to know exactly what he is reacting to. Which test identifies his triggers?","choices":["Methacholine challenge","Repeat spirometry with a bronchodilator","Fractional exhaled nitric oxide","Skin or blood allergy testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Allergy testing, by skin or blood test, is useful to identify allergen triggers. His seasonal and dog-related flares suggest pollen and pet dander, the inhaled allergens that activate mast cells, and testing guides which ones to avoid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 59"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with asthma wakes most nights at about 3 in the morning with cough and wheeze, though she feels fine in the daytime. She asks why her symptoms come at night. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Night symptoms point to vocal fold dysfunction","Bronchial reactivity peaks in the early morning hours","Lying flat causes cardiac asthma","Her rescue inhaler wears off at night"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma symptoms may be worse at night because of circadian variation in bronchomotor tone and bronchial reactivity, which peaks around 3 to 4 in the morning, the time she wakes. Night waking also counts when classifying severity. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has persistent asthma symptoms and frequent rescue inhaler use despite a prescribed inhaled corticosteroid plus long-acting beta-2 agonist. She has not been seen since the prescription 6 months ago. Which step should come first?","choices":["Start long-term oral corticosteroids","Test for steroid-resistant asthma","Check adherence and inhaler technique","Refer for a monoclonal antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-compliance is the most common reason for poor symptom control, and follow-up visits review medications, compliance and correct use before adjusting therapy. Her increased rescue use signals poor control, so adherence and technique are checked first. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 43, 90, 91"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with long-standing heart disease has 3 days of worsening shortness of breath, swelling of both legs, and new wheezing. He has no history of asthma or allergies, and his wheeze began as his heart failure decompensated. Which explanation for his wheezing is most likely?","choices":["Late-onset intrinsic asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Extrinsic allergic asthma","Cardiac asthma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac asthma is wheezing caused by decompensated heart failure. A 74-year-old with heart disease, leg swelling and wheeze that began with the decompensation, and no asthma or atopy, fits it; the heart failure, not the airway, needs treatment. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has daily asthma symptoms and uses albuterol daily, but he wakes at night only once a month and has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. He has some limitation playing pickup basketball. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Daily symptoms, daily short-acting beta-2 agonist use and some limitation of activity are moderate persistent features. Even though his night waking and 85% function are milder, severity is assigned to the most severe category in which any feature occurs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has asthma symptoms throughout every day, uses his rescue inhaler several times a day, wakes with symptoms nearly every night, and is extremely limited in daily activities. His forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 52% predicted. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms throughout the day, rescue use several times a day, night awakenings often 7 times a week, extreme limitation and a 1-second volume below 60% predicted define severe persistent asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
